A set of points of Cantor space is clopen if and only if it is generated by the coordinate sets.

One direction cites the theorem that every generated set is clopen. In the other direction, at each point of the clopen set, the product characterization of openness supplies a finite set of coordinates such that the box pinning those coordinates to the point is contained in the set: a point of the box agrees with the original point on those coordinates, and rewriting by that agreement feeds the second component of the product witness into the product constraint. The chosen boxes cover the set because each point lies in its own box. Closedness yields compactness, and a finite subcover is extracted from the open boxes. The set equals the union of the boxes in that subcover, since the subcover contains the set and each chosen box is contained in the set. That finite union is generated by the coordinate sets.
